# Docker build context — what never reaches an image.
#
# Two images are built from this repository root as their context: `api`
# (Hono + tRPC) and the web bundle, which is built at image build time rather
# than on the Pi (architecture/05 § Serving the web dashboard). Both need the
# whole workspace, because pnpm resolves `packages/*` and `tools/*` from the
# lockfile — so the context is the repo and this file is what keeps it sane.
#
# .dockerignore is NOT .gitignore. Patterns are matched with Go's
# filepath.Match against paths relative to the context root, so a bare
# `node_modules` matches only the one at the root and silently misses
# `packages/db/node_modules`. Every directory pattern below is written `**/`
# for that reason — in a pnpm workspace the nested copies are the majority.

# --- Secrets. This is the reason the file exists. ---
#
# `COPY . .` with .env present bakes POSTGRES_PASSWORD, the session key and the
# model API keys into an image layer. They survive deleting the file in a later
# layer and are readable by anyone who can run `docker history`. §5.3 puts keys
# in the Pi's environment, injected by Compose at run time — never in an image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.crt
**/*.key
**/*.p8
**/*.p12
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/*.mobileprovision

# --- Host dependencies. Never reuse them. ---
#
# pnpm links platform-specific binaries; esbuild's is named explicitly in
# pnpm-workspace.yaml. A macOS/arm64 esbuild copied into a linux/arm64 image
# fails at run time rather than at build time, which is the expensive way to
# discover it. The image runs its own `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
**/node_modules
**/.pnp
**/.pnp.js

# --- Build output. Rebuilt inside the image. ---
**/dist
**/build
**/.next
**/out
**/.expo
**/.expo-shared
**/.turbo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/coverage
**/.nyc_output

# --- Financial data. The same list as .gitignore, for a different reason. ---
#
# These are gitignored, so they are absent from a fresh clone — but the build
# context is the working directory, not the clone, and a working directory is
# exactly where a .mmbak or a downloaded statement sits. This is a public
# repository whose README states it holds no financial data; an image pushed
# anywhere would be the way that stops being true.
**/*.mmbak
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.db
**/*.dump
**/*.sql.gz
**/*.age
**/*.xls
**/*.xlsx
**/*.csv
**/*.ofx
**/*.qif
**/*.mt940
data/
exports/
**/receipts
**/backups
!**/fixtures/**/*.csv

# --- History and local tooling that no image reads ---
#
# .githooks matters more than it looks: it holds private-terms.txt, the
# gitignored list of real names the public specification replaced with
# placeholders. Gitignoring keeps it out of the repository, but the build
# context is the working directory — so without this line the one file that
# must never be published would be copied into an image layer.
.git
.githooks
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.vscode
.editorconfig
biome.json
.idea
# AI assistant rules — source and generated output. Nothing at run time reads
# them, and the generated files repeat the private-data rules verbatim.
.ai-rulez
**/.claude
**/.codex
**/.DS_Store
**/*.log
**/*.orig
**/*.rej

# --- Documentation. Roughly 6,000 lines that nothing at run time reads. ---
#
# TAXONOMY.md is safe to drop: the category tree is compiled into
# packages/db/src/seed/data.ts, not parsed from markdown when seeding runs.
docs/
design/
**/*.md
!README.md

# Deliberately NOT excluded, because the api image needs them on boot:
#   packages/db/drizzle/*.sql        drizzle-kit migrate runs these
#   packages/db/drizzle/meta/*.json  the migration journal
#   pnpm-lock.yaml, pnpm-workspace.yaml, */package.json, tsconfig.base.json
#   tools/  — the lockfile references tools/migrate-mm, so removing it breaks
#            `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` for the whole workspace
